v9k/proofs/ -- host programs that PORTING.md SS8 cites as the correctness
argument for a shipped change.

    make            build and run all of them; nonzero exit if any fails
    make clean

These run on the HOST (the Mac), not on the Victor, and they are not built
by victorow.mak.  They exist because the two things they check cannot be
checked where they actually run:

  vcrc16.c   The VICTOR9K arm of chk3() (upstream edit 17) must compute
             the same CRC as upstream's, on every input, because the far
             end is a stock C-Kermit.  A block check that is fast and
             WRONG fails silently -- the transfer still completes, the
             file is still byte-exact, and the damage is that a corrupt
             packet would be accepted.  Nothing in a transfer test can
             see that.  So the table identity is checked exhaustively
             over all 256 entries and the loop over 20,500
             length-and-fill combinations.  PORTING.md SS16af.

  vznewn.c   Upstream edit 21's FAT-safe unique name.  Two claims that
             are NOT the same: every name it returns must be a legal FAT
             8.3 name (one dot, 1-8 before it, exactly 3 after), and it
             must be the LOWEST free number -- a name that is unique but
             unusable is no better than the ".~1~" it replaced, and one
             that is legal but skips a free slot loses backups silently.
             Plus the failure contract: return 0 AND leave the caller's
             buffer alone, because znewn() then runs upstream's code over
             that same buffer.  6,013 cases, including a sweep of all 999
             numbers over six starting name shapes.  PORTING.md SS16bb.

  vburst.c   The ISR's burst detector only runs when the uPD7201
             overruns.  The chip does not overrun below 38400 (SS16q) and
             MAME cannot drive this machine above 9600 (SS16n), so there
             is no emulator run that reaches the code -- and a bench
             sitting is expensive.  The arithmetic is transcribed here
             and replayed against loss patterns whose answers are known.
             PORTING.md SS16q.


vznewn.c DOES NOT HAVE THE LIMITATION BELOW, AND IT IS THE ONE TO COPY.

Its Makefile rule EXTRACTS v9k_backupname() out of ckvictor.c at build
time into vznewn_gen.h, so the function under test is the function that
ships, by construction rather than by anyone remembering.  The rule fails
loudly if the extraction comes back empty -- a proof compiled against
nothing passes.  What it still supplies by hand is access(), which is the
right thing to fake: the real one is INT 21h, and the test wants to choose
what the directory contains.

The general shape: fake the ENVIRONMENT, extract the CODE.


KNOWN LIMITATION, and it applies to the other two.

vcrc16.c and vburst.c each carry a TRANSCRIPTION of the code they check
against -- vcrc16.c has its own copy of upstream's chk3() and of
crcta[]/crctb[], vburst.c its own copy of the ISR counter update.  So they
prove the port's version agrees with WHAT WAS TRANSCRIBED, not with what
ckcfn2.c and ckvisr.asm contain today.  If upstream's chk3() ever changes,
or the ISR's counters are reworked, these will keep passing and mean less
than they say.

That is the same drift ckvictor.c guards against for the ring size, where
a build-time check fails the compile if ckvisr.asm and the C side disagree.
There is no equivalent here because these are host programs that never see
the target's headers.  The cheap mitigation is the file:line citations in
each transcription's comment -- re-read them when the cited code moves.
